{
  "id": "L-002",
  "title": "One-Sided Limits and When a Limit Exists",
  "atom_ids": ["A-02"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The limit in L-001 was about approach from both sides at once. But a function can behave differently on the two sides of a point, and it is worth being able to say what happens from each side separately. That is what one-sided limits do."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        { "type": "text", "text": "The two sides of a point" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "definition",
      "term": "One-sided limits",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We write "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " and say the left-hand limit is "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " when the values of "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f(x)" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " get arbitrarily close to "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " as "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " approaches "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "a" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " from the left, meaning "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x < a" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". Similarly, "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x) = L" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " names approach from the right, "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x > a" },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "figures/one-sided-limits.png",
      "atom_id": "A-02",
      "caption": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f(x) = x^2" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " near "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x = 2" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " the two sides agree: as "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x \\to 2^-" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " the outputs rise toward 4, and as "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x \\to 2^+" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " they fall toward 4. Because both one-sided limits are 4, the two-sided limit exists and equals 4."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x) = L \\;\\iff\\; \\lim_{x \\to a} f(x) = L",
      "display": true,
      "number": "(1)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        { "type": "text", "text": "The existence test" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The equivalence above is the existence test. If either one-sided limit is missing, or if the two disagree, the two-sided limit does not exist — no matter how well behaved the function looks elsewhere. A jump is the canonical case."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "src": "figures/jump-discontinuity.png",
      "atom_id": "A-02",
      "caption": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "x = 2" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " the left-hand limit is 1 and the right-hand limit is 3. They disagree, so "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to 2} f(x)" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " does not exist, even though "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f(2)" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is defined. The gap between the two branches is the jump."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "widget",
      "name": "plot",
      "version": "1.0",
      "parameters": {
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "left branch, f = 1",
            "points": [
              [-1.0, 1.0], [-0.5, 1.0], [0.0, 1.0], [0.5, 1.0],
              [1.0, 1.0], [1.5, 1.0], [1.8, 1.0], [1.95, 1.0]
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "right branch, f = 3",
            "points": [
              [2.0, 3.0], [2.2, 3.0], [2.5, 3.0], [3.0, 3.0],
              [3.5, 3.0], [4.0, 3.0]
            ]
          }
        ],
        "xlabel": "x",
        "ylabel": "y",
        "title": "A jump at x = 2: the sides disagree"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "important",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When a two-sided limit does not exist, "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\text{DNE}" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is the answer — never a number and never a shrug. A limit that does not exist is information about how the function behaves, not a failure of the problem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "formative",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Suppose "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a^-} f(x) = 5" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " and "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a^+} f(x) = 5" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", but "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f(a) = 12" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". What is "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a} f(x)" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "? State the role the value "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "f(a)" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " plays in your answer."
        }
      ],
      "kind": "free_response"
    }
  ]
}
